-- -*- mode: lua -*-

-- Launchpad map, Albert Gräf <agggraef@gmail.com>, 2 April 2024

-- IMPORTANT: In contrast to the other controllers, this device needs to be
-- connected to its own MIDI port, port #3, on *both* input and output.

-- NOTE: This has only been tested on the Launchpad Pro MK3 so far, so the
-- Launchpad Mini and X might still need some work. Note that the Launchpad
-- Mini doesn't have a programmable drum rack, thus you'll have to use its
-- custom drum layout instead. Other than that, all three devices should work
-- pretty much the same. Of course, the Mini and the X have less buttons,
-- which is why we provide lots of alternative bindings so that all the
-- available functionality is accessible on all devices.

-- The fader setup largely mimics the Launch Control XL map (which see), with
-- the faders and knobs of the XL on the Launchpad's Volume, Pan, and Send A/B
-- fader pages. The CC numbers have been chosen to be compatible (see the Lua
-- table below for the actual numbers if you want to use these for a custom
-- Launchpad layout).

-- LAUNCHPAD FADER BANKS 1-4: These can be accessed using either the
-- corresponding Volume/Pan/Sends/Device buttons in the bottom row of the
-- Launchpad Pro MK3, or with the topmost four scene launch buttons in session
-- mode (labeled Volume, Pan, Send A, and Send B on the Launchpad X, which are
-- also the labels we use in the following, since they nicely match up with
-- the labeling on the Launch Control XL). The latter binding works on all
-- devices, but is only active in session mode.

-- A click of the button activates the corresponding fader page. Another click
-- of the same button then returns you to the most previous (non-fader) view.
-- You can also press and *hold* the button to momentarily switch to the fader
-- page, so that you can quickly change some faders, and revert to the
-- previous view as soon as your release the button again.

-- The available fader pages are as follows:

-- 1: Volume: 1 = pitch hi, 2 = pitch lo, 3 = pattern mode (random, up, down,
-- etc.), 4 = pos/anacrusis (the "scrubbing" control), 5 = tempo, 6 = meter
-- numerator, 7 = meter denominator, 8 = division.

-- 2: Pan = bipolar mod controls

-- 3/4: Send A/B = unipolar min/max controls.

-- The detailed map of all fader pages:

-- 1: [hi]     [lo]   [mode] [pos]     [tempo] [num]     [denom] [divis]
-- 2: [velmod] [pmod] [gain] [gatemod] [hmod]  [prefmod] [smod]  [nmod]
-- 3: [minvel] [pmin] [wmin] [gate]    [hmin]  [pref]    [smin]  [uniq]
-- 4: [maxvel] [pmax] [wmax] [gate]    [hmax]  [pref]    [smax]  [nmax]

-- LAUNCH GRID:

-- Other functions are accessible using the launch grid in session view. The
-- pre-assigned functions are color-coded and laid out in the two pad rows at
-- the bottom, following the same layout as the two button rows on the Launch
-- Control XL:

--   - voice controls -                       - looper controls -
-- [mute] [latch] [bypass]  [      ] [      ] [load] [save] [loop]
--                [raptor]  [rewind]          [prev] [next] [play]

-- NB: The mute, latch, and bypass toggles are also accessible using hardware
-- buttons, see below.

-- PAD CONTROLS: The pads on the launch grid have a sensitivity threshold; a
-- medium touch will trigger them, while a light touch just displays the
-- current binding and value (if any) in the console window. Note that this
-- only works on the Launchpad X and Pro MK3, as the pads on the Launchpad
-- Mini MK3 are not velocity-sensitive.

-- INTELLIGENT MAPPING: The pads can all be mapped using MIDI learn, in which
-- case they light up to indicate that they are bound to a Raptor control.
-- The exact behavior of each pad depends on the type of control it is mapped
-- to. For toggled controls, it acts like a toggle button. Otherwise, it
-- works like a latching fader which inputs a value depending on the velocity
-- (if it is above the threshold) and snaps back to 0 (or the middle position,
-- in the case of a bipolar control) as soon as the pad is released.

-- HARDWARE BUTTONS: The Launchpad's hardware buttons positioned around the
-- launch grid will also be lit up. Some of them can also be MIDI-mapped. The
-- precise set of hardware buttons that you have depends on your type of
-- device, so the button assignments may vary. (However, we try to ensure that
-- all available functionality is available on *all* devices in some way.)

-- Here is a brief rundown of the available functions:

-- The "Play" and "Capture MIDI" buttons on the Launchpad Pro are bound to
-- Raptor's play and loop functions. If you don't have these then you can use
-- the corresponding pads in the session view instead (see above).

-- The left and right arrow buttons can be used to select the current Raptor
-- instance, while the up and down arrow buttons change presets. Note that
-- these buttons are in slightly different locations depending on the
-- Launchpad version. Also note that the arrow keys are only accessible in the
-- session and drum rack views, in other modes they may have a predefined
-- meaning (note mode) or are just unavailable because Novation made it so.

-- On the Launchpad Pro, instances can also be selected using the upper button
-- row at the bottom, and the bottommost row has the following functions:

-- Record Arm/Mute/Solo: These are mapped to the bypass/mute/latch toggles,
-- which are also on the launch grid, albeit in a different order (see above).

-- Volume/Pan/Sends/Device: Access the four fader pages (see above).

-- Stop Clip: Toggle between the standard note view and the drumrack view.
-- (As already mentioned, the drum rack isn't available on the Launchpad Mini,
-- so this button will have no function. But the Mini ships with a custom drum
-- layout which can be used instead.)

-- On all Launchpad versions, the same functionality is replicated on the
-- scene launch buttons in session mode, in a different order and labeled
-- slightly differently, but using the same button colors: Volume, Pan, Send
-- A, Send B, Stop Clip, Mute, Solo, Record Arm. (Note that these precise
-- labels are only on the Launchpad X, but the buttons will work the same also
-- on the Mini and the Pro.)

{
  -- alternative bindings for the mute/latch/bypass controls, uses the mute,
  -- solo, and record arm hardware buttons in the bottom row (LP Pro only)
  [1] = { [33] = { "bypass", true } },
  [2] = { [33] = { "mute", true } },
  [3] = { [33] = { "latch", true } },
  -- alternative bindings for the mute/latch/bypass controls, uses the last
  -- three scene launch buttons (Mute, Solo, Record Arm on the Launchpad X);
  -- these should work on all Launchpad versions
  [39] = { [33] = { "mute", true } },
  [29] = { [33] = { "latch", true } },
  [19] = { [33] = { "bypass", true } },
  -- send A (min value) faders
  [13] = { [37] = "minvel" },
  [14] = { [37] = "pmin" },
  [15] = { [37] = "wmin" },
  [16] = { [37] = "gate" },
  [17] = { [37] = "hmin" },
  [18] = { [37] = "pref" },
  [19] = { [37] = "smin", [33] = { "bypass", true } },
  [20] = { [37] = "uniq" },
  -- send B (max value) faders
  [29] = { [37] = "maxvel", [33] = { "latch", true } },
  [30] = { [37] = "pmax" },
  [31] = { [37] = "wmax" },
  [32] = { [37] = "gate" },
  [33] = { [37] = "hmax" },
  [34] = { [37] = "pref" },
  [35] = { [37] = "smax" },
  [36] = { [37] = "nmax" },
  -- pan (bipolar mod) faders
  [49] = { [37] = "velmod" },
  [50] = { [37] = "pmod" },
  [51] = { [37] = "gain" },
  [52] = { [37] = "gatemod" },
  [53] = { [37] = "hmod" },
  [54] = { [37] = "prefmod" },
  [55] = { [37] = "smod" },
  [56] = { [37] = "nmod" },
  -- volume faders
  [77] = { [37] = "pitchhi" },
  [78] = { [37] = "pitchlo" },
  [79] = { [37] = "mode" },
  [80] = { [37] = "pos" },
  [81] = { [37] = "tempo" },
  [82] = { [37] = "meter-num" },
  [83] = { [37] = "meter-denom" },
  [84] = { [37] = "division" },
  -- predefined pads (session view)
  -- 139, 140 unassigned
  [141] = { [33] = { "raptor", true } },
  [142] = { [33] = { "rewind", true } },
  -- 143 unassigned
  [144] = { [33] = { "loop-prev", true } },
  [145] = { [33] = { "loop-next", true } },
  [146] = { [33] = { "play", true } },
  -- upper row
  [149] = { [33] = { "mute", true } },
  [150] = { [33] = { "latch", true } },
  [151] = { [33] = { "bypass", true } },
  -- 152, 153 unassigned
  [154] = { [33] = { "loop-load", true } },
  [155] = { [33] = { "loop-save", true } },
  [156] = { [33] = { "loop", true } },
}
